WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:07.860
Settle-in, this is unintended consequences episode 7 for May 12, 2026.

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:13.780
Today's story is one of those rare examples where the cure was almost worse than the

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:15.600
disease, at least at first.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:23.480
In the spring of 1928, a young engineer named Thomas Midgley Jr. stood in a laboratory in

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:24.460
Dayton, Ohio.

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:31.180
He demonstrated that a new synthetic gas could be breathed without harm and would not catch

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:34.020
fire even when a lit match was held to it.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:40.820
The compound, dichloro-difloromethane, soon became known as Freon 12.

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:47.820
Within a few decades, it and its chemical cousins had replaced ammonia and sulfur dioxide in

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:50.600
millions of refrigerators and air conditioners.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:57.220
They made safe mechanical cooling available to homes, offices and cars around the world.

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:03.940
Yet the same stability that made these chlorofluora carbons so useful allowed them to drift

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:05.740
intact into the stratosphere.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:12.240
There, they began steadily dismantling the ozone molecules that shield earth from ultraviolet

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:12.860
radiation.

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:19.040
Midgley worked for General Motors' frigidare division at a moment when household refrigeration

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:21.920
was expanding rapidly, but remained dangerous.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:28.900
Leaking ammonia or sulfur dioxide systems had already caused deaths and injuries in homes

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:29.860
and restaurants.

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:34.760
The industry needed a refrigerant that was neither toxic nor flammable.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:41.980
Midgley and his team screened hundreds of compounds before settling on the new class of chlorofluorocarbons.

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:48.740
Their carbon-chlorine fluorine bonds proved remarkably inert under ordinary conditions.

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:54.080
The first commercial units using Freon 12 reached the market in 1930.

00:01:54.880 --> 00:02:00.960
By the late 1930s, Dupont had licensed the technology and begun large-scale production.

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:07.640
At the time, atmospheric chemists knew little about how trace gases behaved once they left

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:08.440
the troposphere.

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:14.820
The stratosphere was still regarded mainly as a stable reservoir of oxygen and nitrogen.

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:20.440
Midgley's solution therefore appeared to be an unqualified public health advance.

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:25.980
It removed one of the last technical barriers to widespread mechanical cooling.

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:32.940
Production of CFCs grew steadily through the 1940s and then accelerated after World War II.

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:38.600
By 1950 more than 100,000 metric tons were manufactured annually.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:44.040
These were chiefly for domestic refrigerators and the new window air conditioners

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:46.220
that were transforming American summers.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:52.920
In the 1960s, the same compounds found new uses as propellants in aerosol cans

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:55.860
and as solvents in the electronics industry.

00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:02.700
Industry literature of the period described CFCs as ideal because they performed reliably

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:07.680
across wide temperature ranges and required no special handling precautions.

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:13.720
A handful of researchers had begun to measure their rising atmospheric concentrations.

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:18.720
Yet these measurements were treated as curiosities rather than warnings.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:24.500
No regulatory bodies saw reason to restrict a substance that had never been linked

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:27.020
to any observable harm on the ground.

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:32.500
The first clear indication that something was a miss came in 1974.

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:39.100
Chemists Mario Molina and F. Sherwood Rowland at the University of California Irvine

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:46.440
published calculations showing that CFC molecules could survive long enough to reach the stratosphere.

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:52.940
Their ultraviolet light would split off chlorine atoms capable of catalytically destroying ozone.

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:57.000
Their paper in nature received modest attention at first.

00:03:57.620 --> 00:04:02.840
The chemistry was still theoretical and ozone levels had not yet shown dramatic change.

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:08.880
Five years later, however, ground-based measurements from the British Antarctic Survey Station

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:15.280
at Halley Bay revealed that October ozone columns above Antarctica had declined by roughly

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:18.200
40% since the late 1970s.

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:23.440
Subsequent aircraft and satellite campaigns confirmed that the depletion was concentrated

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:24.760
in the seasonal hole.

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:30.200
Its size reached more than 20 million square kilometers by 1987.

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:34.900
The causal chain was straightforward once the mechanism was understood.

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:42.660
The very chemical inertness that had made CFCs safe in the lower atmosphere allowed them to accumulate

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:49.740
and then release reactive chlorine at altitudes where ozone is most effective at absorbing ultraviolet light.

00:04:50.420 --> 00:04:52.620
Second order effects followed quickly.

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:58.460
Increased surface ultraviolet radiation raised estimates of additional skin cancer cases

00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:00.360
in mid-latitude populations.

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:07.300
The seasonal Antarctic hole altered expectations about how quickly the atmosphere might recover

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:09.260
even after emissions stopped.

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:18.160
A third-order consequence emerged when governments and companies began searching for substitutes.

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:25.460
The first generation of replacements, hydrochloro-florocarbons, still contained chlorine and therefore

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:27.480
required a second round of controls.

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:33.720
The scientific consensus solidified rapidly after the 1985 discovery.

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:42.240
In September 1987, diplomats, meeting in Montreal produced the first binding international agreement

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:50.460
to phase out ozone-depleting substances, developed countries committed to a 50% reduction in CFC production

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:56.180
by 1998. Subsequent amendments accelerated the schedule.

00:05:57.140 --> 00:06:02.740
Production of the main CFCs ended in industrialized nations by 1996.

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:09.580
The Montreal Protocol is widely credited with preventing far larger ozone losses.

00:06:10.580 --> 00:06:16.660
Satellite records now show that the Antarctic ozone hole has begun a slow, uneven recovery.

00:06:17.420 --> 00:06:22.680
Full restoration to 1980 levels is not expected until roughly 2070.

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:27.200
The transition to substitutes was not without its own complications.

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:35.160
Hydro-florocarbons adopted in the 1990s proved to be potent greenhouse gases.

00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:40.800
This prompted the 2016 Cagalia amendment to address their climate impact.

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:47.480
Yet the original decision-makers in the 1930s could not have anticipated either the atmospheric

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:51.960
chemistry or the institutional machinery that eventually addressed it.

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:59.100
One clear principle is that chemical stability, while desirable for safety and performance on the

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:05.540
ground, can become a liability once a substance enters a region where different reaction pathways

00:07:05.540 --> 00:07:13.520
dominate. A second is that global commons' problems often require monitoring systems capable of

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:18.880
detecting slow, cumulative changes long before local effects become obvious.

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:25.420
Finally, the Montreal experience suggests that international agreements can succeed

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:33.080
when the underlying science is clear. The number of major producers is small and technically feasible

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:40.120
substitutes exist. The forward-looking question the story leaves us with is how many other widely

00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:47.060
used compounds we are releasing today, whose atmospheric lifetimes and reaction products we still

00:07:47.060 --> 00:07:54.200
understand only incompletely. That wraps today's case. The lessons travel further when you share them.

00:07:54.660 --> 00:07:58.600
Send this episode to someone who'd find it useful. We'll see you tomorrow.

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:06.140
This episode used AI voice synthesis of my voice. Editorial selection and analysis are my own.
